  "response": "## Reading the graph first\n\nTwo different numbers are both called \"hotspot,\" and they want opposite treatments:\n\n- **Fan-in** = how far a mistake here travels. `src/utils.ts` (18 direct / 29 transitive) and `src/plugin.ts` (10 / 29) are the amplifiers. → *Don't move the surface; fix the insides.*\n- **Fan-out** = how much you must hold in context to edit safely. `src/index.ts` (17, vs p99 cutoff 11) and `src/esbuild/index.ts` (11) are the orchestrators. → *Split them; that's where blast radius is created, not suffered.*\n\nOne correction to the raw counts before ranking: several `large_function` findings are **the same code at different nesting depths**, so the 8 high-severity findings are fewer than 8 refactors. `build` (167–459) ⊃ `<anonymous>` (183–456) ⊃ `mainTasks` (260–453) ⊃ `buildAll` (291–365) + `startWatcher` (367–445) — six findings, one body. Same for `postcssPlugin` (6–145) ⊃ `setup` (18–143), and `sveltePlugin` (9–124) ⊃ `setup` (17–122) ⊃ anon (31–121). All 8 high-severity findings in this scan are God Functions; none are correctness bugs.\n\n## Triage order\n\n**1. `src/index.ts` — do this first**\n\nBest payoff-to-risk ratio in the repo. It is simultaneously the largest file (459 lines, 1.5× threshold, `crime_00078`) and the highest fan-out module (17 imports vs p99 of 11, `crime_00081`) — it wires nearly every other module together. Six stacked function findings collapse into one refactor: `build` at 293 lines (4.9× threshold, conf 0.95), `mainTasks` 194, `normalizeOptions` 96, `startWatcher` 79, `buildAll` 75.\n\nThe decisive number is the asymmetry: **fan-out 17, fan-in 4.** You get to dismantle the repo's biggest complexity concentration while only 4 files can see the change, and `test_gap: 0` means there's real coverage to refactor against. Extracting `normalizeOptions`, `startWatcher`, and `mainTasks` into their own modules also decomposes that fan-out 17 into several honest, smaller dependency sets. Fold in the `name_behavior_mismatch` on `build`/`buildAll` (`crime_00014`, severity 0.55 — \"safe-sounding name, calls `removeFiles`\") while you're in there.\n\n**2. `src/esbuild/index.ts` — `runEsbuild`**\n\n237 lines, **4.0× the threshold**, confidence 0.95 (`crime_00056`), reading **38 distinct properties** off one untyped `options` bag (`crime_00017`). Fan-out 11 sits exactly at the p99 cutoff. And it costs almost nothing to fix: **1 direct importer**, 7 transitive, `test_gap: 0`. Second-biggest logic pile in the repo behind `index.ts`, with the smallest coupling bill of any hotspot.\n\n**3. `src/plugin.ts` — `buildFinished` only, not the module**\n\nOnly *medium* severity at 103 lines, but it carries the second-highest blast radius in `src/`: **10 direct importers, 29 transitive** (`crime_00052`, blast 0.45), and `test_gap: 0.75` — 29 files sit downstream of an untested 103-line method.\n\nScope this narrowly. The module-level finding (`crime_00067`) notes **9 of its 10 importers take types only** — that coupling is compile-time, and being depended on is a shared type module's job. So: extract helpers out of the method body, change nothing about the exported type surface.\n\n**4. `src/utils.ts` — split second, fix the swallowed error now**\n\nHighest fan-in in the repo: **18 direct importers, 29 transitive**, blast 0.46 (`crime_00079`), plus 424 lines (`crime_00074`). It's the biggest amplifier and therefore the *most expensive* thing to restructure — the tool itself rates `split_file` as medium risk, and here that risk lands on 18 import sites.\n\nSplit the work:\n- **Now, cheap:** `jsoncParse` at `src/utils.ts:135` catches with no binding and returns `{}` (`crime_00049`). Same 0.46 blast radius — a malformed config silently becomes an empty object for 29 downstream files, and the error object isn't even available to the handler. Small, low-risk, high leverage.\n- **Later, deliberate:** split by responsibility behind a re-export barrel so the 18 importers don't churn.\n\n**5. `src/esbuild/postcss.ts`**\n\nHigh severity — `postcssPlugin` 140 lines + `setup` 126 (2.3×/2.1×, conf 0.93/0.91) — and the worst agent-risk combination in the scan: `test_gap: 0.75` **and** 3 return shapes at **0% key overlap** (`{ namespace, path }` vs `{ contents, loader }`, `crime_00001`/`crime_00002`). An agent editing this has neither tests nor a return contract to anchor on. Cheap to do (1 direct importer), but it's a leaf — no blast-radius payoff, so it ranks below the load-bearing work. Add the discriminated union before touching the body.\n\n## Leave alone\n\n- **`src/log.ts`** (`crime_00066`) — fan-in 9, **fan-out 0**, sitting exactly *at* the p95 cutoff, with no size or complexity finding attached to it. A pure leaf logger with 9 importers is doing its job. Connectivity alone is not a defect. No action.\n- **`src/rollup.ts`** (`crime_00076`) — tempting, because `getRollupConfig` is 122 lines at high severity plus a false-identity name (`setSilent`, `removeFiles`), a 10-property option bag, and a swallowed error. But **blast_radius 0, fan-in 0, 0 transitive importers** — nothing imports this file. Zero leverage against the stated objective. Good cleanup, wrong list.\n- **`test/utils.ts`** (`crime_00082`, `crime_00020`) — fan-in 9 with an unbounded `Promise.all` over `Object.keys(files)`. Test harness: N is fixture size, not production data, and 9 importers means any change ripples through the suite for no production gain. Optional one-line concurrency cap; otherwise ignore.\n- **`src/api-extractor.ts`** (`crime_00070`) — fan-out 6, exactly at p95, 1 importer. `rollupDtsFiles` is medium at 85 lines. Defer.\n- **`src/cli-main.ts`** — high severity (`main`, 153 lines, conf 0.95) but blast radius 0.15 (2/2). Not blast-radius work. It has `test_gap: 0.75`, so when you do touch it, write a test first.\n- **`swc-target.ts` / `terser.ts`** at 62 lines — **1.0× the threshold**. Rounding noise. Leave.\n\n## One caveat on the objective\n\nNothing above is a correctness bug — the ranking optimizes for *reducing how far a future wrong edit travels*, which is what you asked for. If you also want the risk *today* reduced, the 28 `weak_test_signal` findings are the real story: 15 tests with **0 assertions** (`test/experimental-dts.test.ts`, `test/graphql.test.ts`, `test/shims.test.ts`). That's a separate workstream, but it's why `cli-main.ts`, `postcss.ts`, `rollup.ts`, and `plugin.ts` all carry `test_gap: 0.75` — and why picks 1 and 2, which have coverage, are the safe places to start.",
